<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!-- The login page.

         TWO templates, and the split is the whole point. Read this before
         moving anything back into one.

         `web.login_layout` (web/views/webclient_templates.xml:110) is the CARD:
         a t-call to web.frontend_layout wrapping a card > card-body containing
         the stock logo, `<t t-out="0"/>` for the page content, and a footer.

         `web.login` (line 136) is the CONTENT poured into that t-out.

         WEBSITE DESTROYS THE CARD. website/views/website_templates.xml:1622
         inherits web.login_layout at priority 20 with

             <xpath expr="t[@t-call]" position="replace">
                 <t t-call="website.layout">
                     <div class="oe_website_login_container" t-out="0"/>
                 </t>
             </xpath>

         — it replaces the ENTIRE t-call subtree, so the card, the card-body and
         the stock logo div all cease to exist. Anything we xpath into them
         disappears with them. That is exactly what happened: with `website`
         installed the logo and the support line vanished silently.

         A PRIORITY BUMP DOES NOT FIX IT, and this was measured rather than
         assumed: at priority 50 (applying after website's 20) the upgrade did
         NOT error, but the login tests went from 2 failures to 4 — worse. Our
         specs then operate on a subtree that website has already thrown away.

         The fix WAS to stop anchoring the branding in the contested layout at
         all: everything that had to survive moved into `web.login`, whose
         content is passed through `t-out="0"` into whichever wrapper wins — the
         stock card, or website's. The login FORM is the anchor because a login
         page cannot stop containing its own login form; it is the one node here
         that cannot be optioned away.

         THAT IS NOW HALF THE STORY. `sapian_theme_website` deactivates
         website's inheritance outright (its views/login_layout.xml explains
         why: the login page was being served inside the marketing site's
         header and footer), so the card is no longer contested — it is the
         same card in both configurations.

         The logo and the support line STAY on the form. They are content, they
         read correctly where the form is, and moving them would be churn for
         its own sake. The ATTRIBUTION moved back to the card footer, because
         the form anchor reached /web/login alone while the card footer also
         reaches /web/signup and /web/reset_password — see the comment on that
         xpath below. Which is the general rule for this file: anchor a thing
         where every page that needs it will find it, and say which pages those
         are.

         Matched on `contains(@t-attf-class, 'oe_login_form')` and NOT on
         `hasclass('oe_login_form')`. The class is COMPUTED —
         `t-attf-class="oe_login_form #{'' if login else 'd-none'}"` — so there
         is no static `class` attribute for hasclass() to see, and that xpath
         fails to locate the node at INSTALL time, not at render time. Measured:
         it aborts the install with "Element cannot be located in parent view".

         `web.login_layout` is still inherited, for exactly one job: deleting
         the stock logo so we do not render two. When website is installed that
         deletion is moot (the node is gone anyway) and harmlessly discarded.

         The sign-in button needs no rule here — it is styled at ASSET level in
         sapian_frontend.scss, which is why the button colour survived `website`
         when these two template features did not. See README.md. -->

    <template id="login_layout_sapian" inherit_id="web.login_layout" priority="10"
              name="SapianERP login branding (stock logo removal)">
        <!-- Remove Odoo's unconditional company logo. `/web/binary/company_logo`
             always returns an image — Odoo's own when the company has set none —
             so leaving it would greet a prospect with someone else's branding on
             a fresh database. The replacement lives in `login_sapian` below,
             which checks the field and degrades to the company NAME instead.

             priority=10 is DELIBERATE and must stay BELOW website's 20
             (website/views/website_templates.xml:1622). This view must apply
             while the stock logo still exists; website then replaces the whole
             card and this deletion becomes moot, which is fine. Applying AFTER
             website means xpath'ing into a subtree that no longer exists — and
             that is not theory: at priority 50 the login page returned HTTP 500
             with "Element ... cannot be located in parent view".

             Explicit rather than relying on Odoo's default of 16, because a
             stored priority is NOT reset when the XML stops specifying it. A
             database that ever carried a different value keeps it through every
             subsequent upgrade — measured, on the database where the 50 was
             tried. Stating the number is what lets such a database self-heal. -->

        <xpath expr="//div[hasclass('text-center')][hasclass('pb-3')]" position="replace"/>

        <!-- "Powered by Odoo", removed.

             web/views/webclient_templates.xml:128 hardcodes

                 <a href="https://www.odoo.com?utm_source=db&amp;utm_medium=auth"
                    target="_blank">Powered by <span>Odoo</span></a>

             in the login card's footer, so the page a client opens every
             morning attributes their ERP to somebody else and links away to a
             competitor's sales funnel. See README.md ("Odoo attribution") for
             the licensing position: `web` is LGPL-3, which requires no
             attribution in a user interface, and the LGPL conveys no trademark
             licence either way — so the link is Odoo's marketing, not a legal
             notice, and removing it removes marketing.

             Matched by HREF rather than by position: the surrounding footer div
             also holds the "Manage Databases" link, which is a real feature and
             must survive. Nothing else in this template points at odoo.com.

             OURS REPLACES IT, IN PLACE — and that is a change. It used to be
             anchored on the login form in `login_sapian` below, because
             `website` replaced this whole card and anything left here vanished
             with it. `sapian_theme_website` now deactivates that inheritance
             (see its views/login_layout.xml), so the card exists in every
             configuration and the workaround has outlived its cause.

             Moving it back is not tidying. On the form it reached /web/login
             only, because /web/signup and /web/reset_password extend
             web.login_layout and never render `web.login`. With the website
             footer gone those two pages had NO attribution at all — measured,
             as a failure of `test_the_other_auth_pages_keep_theirs`, which is
             the assertion that caught it. Here, one line serves all three. -->
        <xpath expr="//a[contains(@href, 'odoo.com')]" position="replace">
            <span class="o_sapian_powered">
                <a href="https://sapiantech.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                    <t t-call="sapian_theme.mark"/>
                    <span>Powered by SapianERP</span>
                </a>
            </span>
        </xpath>
    </template>

    <template id="login_sapian" inherit_id="web.login"
              name="SapianERP login branding (content)">

        <!-- Logo, or the company name when there is none. Anchored BEFORE the
             login form, which puts it exactly where the stock logo was inside
             the card, and keeps it at the top of website's container too. -->
        <xpath expr="//form[contains(@t-attf-class, 'oe_login_form')]" position="before">
            <t t-set="sapian_company"
               t-value="env['res.company'].sudo().search([], order='id', limit=1)"/>
            <div class="text-center pb-3 border-bottom mb-4">
                <img t-if="sapian_company.logo"
                     t-attf-src="/web/binary/company_logo{{ '?dbname='+db if db else '' }}"
                     alt="Logo" style="max-height:120px; max-width: 100%; width:auto"/>
                <h1 t-else="" class="h4 fw-bold mb-0 text-truncate"
                    t-esc="sapian_company.name or ''"/>
            </div>
        </xpath>

        <!-- Support contact line.

             Configurable, never hard-coded: the system parameter
             `sapian_theme.support_contact` holds whatever the deployment wants
             to show — a phone number, a Telegram handle, an email. It is
             deliberately free text: support channels differ per client and per
             sector, and this module is horizontal.

             Empty state renders NOTHING — no empty box, no stray separator.
             That is what the t-if on the whole block is for. -->
        <xpath expr="//form[contains(@t-attf-class, 'oe_login_form')]" position="after">
            <t t-set="sapian_support"
               t-value="env['ir.config_parameter'].sudo().get_param('sapian_theme.support_contact')"/>
            <div t-if="sapian_support" class="text-center small text-muted mt-3">
                <span>Support:</span>
                <span t-esc="sapian_support"/>
            </div>
        </xpath>

        <!-- THE ATTRIBUTION IS NO LONGER HERE. It moved back into the card
             footer in `login_layout_sapian` above, where it replaces "Powered
             by Odoo" in place and therefore reaches /web/signup and
             /web/reset_password as well as /web/login. The reason it was ever
             anchored on the form — `website` destroying the card — was removed
             by `sapian_theme_website`. Left as a note rather than deleted
             silently, because the form anchor is the kind of thing that looks
             arbitrary and gets "restored" by someone reading the git history
             without the cause. -->

    </template>

</odoo>
